Instruction: Read the segment of the Strategic Acquisition and Advisory Agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an Acquisition Proposal is classified as Superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Strategic Acquisition and Advisory Agreement: SECTION 9.7. Comprehensive Proposal Evaluation. (a) Notwithstanding any other provision herein, the Strategic Review Committee may, at any time before final approval by the Board, classify an Acquisition Proposal as Superior only if it fulfills all of the following conditions: 
    (1) A detailed financial analysis demonstrating favorable monetary returns and liquidity ratios;
    (2) Consultation with external legal counsel to ensure compliance with fiduciary duties and applicable regulations;
    (3) An independent strategic review by market analysts to assess competitive positioning and long-term sustainability; 
    (4) A risk assessment report prepared by external risk management advisors highlighting non-financial risks including operational and reputational factors; and
    (5) A negotiated period with the initiating party to address any identified deficiencies in strategic alignment or governance practices, which, if unresolved, shall mandate re-evaluation of the proposal.

Clause (b) Further, in any instance where there is a material alteration in non-financial metrics, such as risk or strategic alignment, the Committee is required to re-assess the classification irrespective of the underlying financial data provided. (Pages 101-102)
