Question: In determining whether an Acquisition Proposal qualifies as superior, is the 'financial point of view' the only factor considered?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: Section 8.03 - Comprehensive Evaluation of Proposals. 
(a) The Company Board shall assess any Acquisition Proposal through a holistic review process that includes, but is not limited to, an analysis of strategic alignment, risk profile, and the fiduciary responsibilities owed to its shareholders. 
(b) A Proposal shall be officially designated as a Superior Proposal only if all of the following conditions are met: 
    (i) The Proposal features materially advantageous financial terms; 
    (ii) The Proposal is subject to a series of mandatory negotiations, during which the Company invites Parent to propose modifications; 
    (iii) The Company, in consultation with both its designated financial advisor and outside legal counsel, confirms that the Proposal meets all statutory fiduciary standards; 
    (iv) Any modification to the financial terms requires an automatic reevaluation period, during which additional factors such as market conditions and potential operational synergies are thoroughly examined; 
    (v) The entire review process is undertaken in good faith and all decisions are comprehensively documented.
